    "Model exhibits severe underestimation during high-volatility regimes; when true outcomes spike >10% above the last known price (e.g., Example 2, 3, 4), forecasts remain flat or trend slowly upward while reality surges.",
    "Forecast horizon is biased toward mean reversion; models predict a steady linear uptrend even when sentiment history shows mixed signals and recent volatility indicates a potential crash or consolidation.",
    "Sentiment spikes (1.0) in the last 3 days of history correlate with immediate price drops in true outcomes, yet model forecasts ignore this reversal signal and continue projecting growth.",
    "Model fails to capture 'mean reversion' from extremes; after a sharp rise in true prices, the forecast continues its upward trajectory instead of correcting downward toward the historical average.",
    "Forecast error is maximized when sentiment history contains conflicting signals (e.g., recent 1.0 followed by -1.0); models treat these as noise rather than volatility indicators."
